{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/pulls/3069","id":4559152127,"node_id":"PR_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAABD78j_w","html_url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/3069","diff_url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/3069.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/3069.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/issues/3069","number":3069,"state":"open","locked":false,"title":"Bind a compared cell verdict to the exact attempt it was computed from","user":{"login":"rrnewton","id":274582,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/274582?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton","html_url":"https://github.com/rrnewton","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"[hermit2, review-3066, unresolved, devbig014, role=impl]\n\nPlain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nA terminal cell verdict said a comparison happened and never said which observed\nattempt produced it. The evidence a verdict was computed from was therefore not\nnamed by the verdict, and the only way to reach it was to guess: match on a\ntimestamp, an ordering, an equal result, or a plausible retry. That guess does\nnot work. Across 22,502 compared verdicts, 13,201 have exactly one matching\npublished validate event, 9,276 have none, and 25 have two retry events -- so\nfor more than a third of them the guess has no answer at all, and for 25 it has\ntwo answers and no way to choose.\n\nThis makes the verdict name its own evidence. A compared verdict now carries the\nexact series event identity of the attempt it read, written by the producer at\nthe moment it selects that attempt.\n\nTask: bind_terminal_cellverdicts_to\n\nTHE IDENTITY IS DERIVED, NOT COPIED, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN. A published series\nevent_id is a pure digest over {run_id, producer, cell, tree, run_index, and\nattempt when the row carries one}. Measured against the whole published corpus\nrather than a sample: 71,607 of 71,607 rows recompute from their own payload,\n0 differ, including all 1,385 attempt-bearing rows. So the binding computes the\nidentity from its own five coordinates instead of carrying a string a producer\ncould get wrong. A binding cannot name an event that its own coordinates do not\nproduce, which is what makes a wrong cell, a wrong attempt, a foreign run and a\nforeign tree fail BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by a lookup that has to be\nremembered.\n\nThree producer rules are folded into one constructor, each measured rather than\nread off the source, because getting any of them wrong yields a well-formed\nbinding naming an event nobody published:\n\n  * A validate row's run_index IS the cell result's attempt ordinal. Over 69,907\n    validate rows the only observed pairs are (1,1) and (2,2).\n  * A validate row carries the separate `attempt` key -- which changes the\n    digest -- if and only if it has no-verdict evidence: 69,477 without it, 430\n    with it, no exception. A compared verdict has a verdict, so its digest omits\n    the key.\n  * An attempt-bearing row is never collapsed, so collapsing cannot stale a\n    predicted run_index.\n\nForward-only, with no backfill. Schema 7 and the schema-8 lift leave the field\nabsent, which is how an old row says UNBOUND; there is no inference from\ntimestamps, ordering or a guessed retry that could recover the identity, and\nevery one of those would manufacture a foreign key rather than record one.\n\nTwo guards, because the field alone is fail-open -- a producer that simply\nstopped writing it would emit rows indistinguishable from pre-binding ones.\n`require_bound_compared_cells` refuses a compared verdict with no binding or a\nbinding that disagrees with its run, tree, cell or attempt. `bound_event_ids`\nREFUSES a duplicate rather than deduplicating it, because one attempt cannot be\nthe evidence for two cells and folding it would count one measurement twice.\n\nThe artifact row now records `selected_attempt`. That is what lets the artifact\nverifier RE-DERIVE the binding independently instead of reading it back out of\nthe row it is supposed to be checking -- without it the digest-bound comparison\nwould compare the binding against itself and pass for any value.\n\nThe producer takes the attempt from the SAME row the verdict came from. The\nparity path shares one `selected_attempt_index` with `candidate_verdict`, so the\nverdict and the binding beside it cannot come to name different attempts.\n\nDeterminism\n\nThe binding is a pure function of already-recorded fields: no clock, no\nordering, no iteration-order dependence, no host state. SHA-256 over a canonical\nJSON object whose key order is fixed and written out explicitly rather than\ninherited from field declaration order, so the same attempt yields the same\nidentity on every machine and every read. No comparison, tolerance, comparator\nor scheduling behaviour is touched, and no existing expectation was edited to\nadmit the change.\n\nLinux Semantics\n\nNo guest syscall, process, signal or exit-status behaviour is affected. This is\nvalidation-evidence bookkeeping outside the guest entirely.\n\nValidation\n\ncargo test -p hermit-manifest-plan: 248 passed, 0 failed.\ncargo clippy -p hermit-manifest-plan --all-targets -- -D warnings: clean.\ncargo fmt --all -- --check: clean.\n\nEight new binding tests. Six are REAL published (coordinates -> event_id)\nvectors taken verbatim from the tracked series shards, three carrying an attempt\nand three not, so both digest encodings are pinned against data rather than\nagainst this code's own opinion of itself.\n\nMeasured in both directions, four controls, each run rather than asserted:\n\n  verify_against short-circuits to Ok          negative test fails\n  verify_self_consistent short-circuits to Ok  negative test fails\n  the digest loses its `series-` prefix        2 fail, incl. the real vectors\n  the producer writes no binding               producer test fails, naming\n                                               \"compared verdict left unbound\"\n\nAnd a control in the other direction, so the negative tests are not passing\nmerely because everything fails: a correctly rebuilt binding for each perturbed\naxis -- run, cell, attempt -- is ACCEPTED.\n\nWhole-suite A/B under identical conditions. The `scripts/validate.rs` test\nbinary reports 73 passed / 5 failed both at pristine dc3a873b2 and with this\nchange, and `diff` of the two failing sets is empty. Those 5 are pre-existing on\nmain and none is this change's.\n\nNot claimed, and named rather than implied: the compatibility website still\ncounts comparisons through the older heuristic join -- the binding it needs now\nexists, but switching it over lives in the parent repository. And the acceptance\nrun (\"a new authentic validation run proves every compared terminal verdict\nresolves\") is not this change; this makes the rows bindable and enforced, it is\nnot itself that run.\n\n\n---\n\n## Correction at c4b692ada, and it matters\n\n**Everything above was written for the first commit, and one of its claims was\nfalse as landed on the branch.** It says \"Two guards, because the field alone is\nfail-open — a producer that simply stopped writing it would emit rows\nindistinguishable from pre-binding ones.\" The guards existed. **Nothing called\nthem.** A grep over the whole tree found one occurrence of\n`require_bound_compared_cells`: the doc comment asserting it was the guard.\n\nSo at `3c877f2bd` the field really was fail-open, and the paragraph claiming\notherwise is exactly the shape this change exists to remove — a value that reads\nas assurance and carries none. It is left standing above rather than edited out,\nbecause a quietly corrected record is worse than a visible one.\n\nA second fault explains why the first was not obvious: the guard was written on\n`CellResultsEvidence`, the lifted 6/7/8/9 shape, while the binding is carried by\n`CellResultsEvidenceV10` — and `cell_results_evidence()` returns `None` for\nschema 10. It could never have seen a bound row even if something had called it.\n\n`c4b692ada` moves the guard onto the type that holds bindings and invokes it\nfrom `CellResultsEvidenceV10::validate_for_row`, which is on the live decode\npath through `schema10_cell_results`. It also makes the guard symmetric: a\nverdict that compared nothing must not carry a binding either.\n\nThe new test goes through the live decode rather than calling the guard, because\ncalling it directly is what the first commit effectively did and it proved\nnothing. Control: with the call removed and nothing else changed, the row whose\nbinding was deleted decodes successfully and the test fails.\n\n⚠️ And the first attempt at that control silently did nothing — the patch matched\nno text, the suite ran against an unmodified tree, and it reported green. Same\nclass of defect as the uninvoked guard, caught only because the patch script\nasserted loudly. Recorded rather than tidied away.\n\n`cargo test -p hermit-manifest-plan`: 249 passed, up from 248. Clippy and fmt\nclean.\n","created_at":"2026-09-17T13:47:46Z","updated_at":"2026-09-17T14:59:51Z","closed_at":null,"merged_at":null,"merge_commit_sha":"cb2b34065769023b6b4870813e13bb96950229b2","assignees":[],"requested_reviewers":[],"requested_teams":[],"labels":[],"milestone":null,"draft":false,"commits_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/pulls/3069/commits","review_comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/pulls/3069/comments","review_comment_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/pulls/comments{/number}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/issues/3069/comments","statuses_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/statuses/9cec06dcc9d8acf4828b72f73d9bb1abd1f01ddc","head":{"label":"rrnewton:review-3066/bind-cell-verdict-to-its-attempt","ref":"review-3066/bind-cell-verdict-to-its-attempt","sha":"9cec06dcc9d8acf4828b72f73d9bb1abd1f01ddc","user":{"login":"rrnewton","id":274582,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/274582?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton","html_url":"https://github.com/rrnewton","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"repo":{"id":563646124,"node_id":"R_kgDOIZiOrA","name":"hermit","full_name":"rrnewton/hermit","private":false,"owner":{"login":"rrnewton","id":274582,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/274582?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton","html_url":"https://github.com/rrnewton","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"html_url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit","description":"Hermit launches linux x86_64 programs in a special, hermetically isolated sandbox to control their execution. Hermit translates normal, nondeterministic behavior, into deterministic, repeatable behavior. This can be used for various applications, including replay-debugging, reproducible artifacts, chaos mode concurrency testing and bug analysis.","fork":true,"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit","forks_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/forks","keys_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/keys{/key_id}","collaborators_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/collaborators{/collaborator}","teams_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/teams","hooks_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/hooks","issue_events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/issues/events{/number}","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/events","assignees_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/assignees{/user}","branches_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/branches{/branch}","tags_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/tags","blobs_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/git/blobs{/sha}","git_tags_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/he